WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.134 --> 00:00:14.836
Snow sprayed as Kai swerved around a tree.

2
00:00:16.478 --> 00:00:19.320
His skis carved two neat lines down the trail.

3
00:00:21.022 --> 00:00:26.327
It was only an intermediate trail, but it was more difficult than the intermediate trails Kai skied back

4
00:00:26.367 --> 00:00:27.128
home in Vermont.

5
00:00:28.928 --> 00:00:32.531
When he reached the bottom, he found his best friend, Zinnia, waiting for him.

6
00:00:34.232 --> 00:00:35.473
"She beat me again!"

7
00:00:35.873 --> 00:00:36.414
Kai thought.

8
00:00:37.375 --> 00:00:38.716
He didn't let that bother him though.

9
00:00:40.237 --> 00:00:45.000
Zinnia's aunt and uncle owned the ski resort, so it made sense that she was an accomplished skier.

10
00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:48.523
"You call that an intermediate trail?"

11
00:00:48.903 --> 00:00:49.404
Kai said.

12
00:00:49.424 --> 00:00:51.665
"That was hard!"

13
00:00:52.846 --> 00:00:53.687
Zinnia laughed.

14
00:00:56.512 --> 00:00:59.034
"Every ski resort has its own rating system.

15
00:01:00.955 --> 00:01:05.239
That was an intermediate trail only compared to the other trails at this resort.

16
00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:09.542
Plus, you're used to Vermont's smaller mountains.

17
00:01:10.643 --> 00:01:12.304
We're in Colorado now."

18
00:01:12.324 --> 00:01:15.767
"Right," Kai said.

19
00:01:15.787 --> 00:01:18.769
"These mountains are huge!"

20
00:01:18.789 --> 00:01:22.672
"Come on," Zinnia said, taking off.

21
00:01:23.884 --> 00:01:27.305
"Let's check out a more advanced slope—Cliffhanger!"

22
00:01:29.326 --> 00:01:30.267
Kai gulped.

23
00:01:30.287 --> 00:01:34.008
"The intermediate trail was already hard," he thought.

24
00:01:34.028 --> 00:01:41.071
"Cliffhanger will probably be way more intense than the advanced slopes that I'm used to back home!"

25
00:01:43.652 --> 00:01:46.093
Kai and Zinnia rode the chairlift up the mountain.

26
00:01:48.034 --> 00:01:54.469
When they got to the top, Kai was a little disappointed, but also relieved—Cliffhanger was closed for maintenance.

27
00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:57.410
"Hey, Zinnia!"

28
00:01:57.730 --> 00:01:58.771
A woman walked toward them.

29
00:01:59.971 --> 00:02:00.751
"Hi, Aunt Kate!"

30
00:02:01.091 --> 00:02:03.432
Zinnia said before introducing Kai to her aunt.

31
00:02:03.452 --> 00:02:08.334
"Sorry about Cliffhanger," Aunt Kate said.

32
00:02:08.354 --> 00:02:11.676
"It's closed for grooming right now."

33
00:02:13.256 --> 00:02:14.357
Kai was confused.

34
00:02:14.377 --> 00:02:18.138
"Grooming?" Aunt Kate smiled.

35
00:02:18.158 --> 00:02:22.040
"That's what we call it when we get trails ready for skiers.

36
00:02:23.404 --> 00:02:26.645
We usually do it at night, but last night was too busy.

37
00:02:27.885 --> 00:02:31.586
Our top priority is always getting the easier trails groomed first.

38
00:02:32.627 --> 00:02:39.448
Fewer people use the advanced trails, so we don't usually bother with them, but they've gotten pretty icy."

39
00:02:40.669 --> 00:02:45.190
Kai looked down the slope and saw machines blowing out thick, powdery white clouds.

40
00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:48.051
"What do those machines do?"

41
00:02:48.071 --> 00:02:52.212
"They're making artificial snow," Aunt Kate said.

42
00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:56.301
"Artificial? Man-made snow?"

43
00:02:56.741 --> 00:02:57.442
Kai blinked.

44
00:02:57.462 --> 00:02:59.843
"What's it made out of?"

45
00:02:59.863 --> 00:03:05.385
"It's still just frozen water, like real snow," Zinnia said.

46
00:03:05.405 --> 00:03:13.268
"The ski resort can only earn money a few months each year," Aunt Kate explained, "and some winters

47
00:03:13.328 --> 00:03:14.949
we don't get enough snowfall.

48
00:03:16.469 --> 00:03:20.271
We can't afford to close down while we wait for more snow to fall.

49
00:03:21.511 --> 00:03:22.932
So we make our own."

50
00:03:25.084 --> 00:03:26.565
"How do the snowmakers work?"

51
00:03:26.946 --> 00:03:27.586
Kai asked.

52
00:03:27.606 --> 00:03:32.010
"The process starts with water," Aunt Kate said.

53
00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:38.575
"A special protein that helps ice crystals form is added to the water, which is then pumped up

54
00:03:38.635 --> 00:03:39.456
to the machines.

55
00:03:41.097 --> 00:03:46.482
Each machine has several nozzles that shoot water, along with compressed air, into a large fan.

56
00:03:47.963 --> 00:03:53.428
The mixture of water and air sprays out of the fan, freezes, and then falls as snow."

57
00:03:55.252 --> 00:03:59.014
Kai saw a couple of large red vehicles pushing snow downhill.

58
00:03:59.034 --> 00:04:03.097
"Those are our snowcats," Aunt Kate said.

59
00:04:03.117 --> 00:04:10.221
"They groom the slopes by pushing snow into place and smoothing it out, creating a perfect surface for

60
00:04:10.281 --> 00:04:15.645
skiers." "So you can have snow any time of year?"

61
00:04:15.925 --> 00:04:16.625
Kai asked.

62
00:04:16.645 --> 00:04:20.648
"Not exactly," Aunt Kate said.

63
00:04:21.688 --> 00:04:26.390
"The temperature still needs to be around freezing for the machines to work properly and efficiently.

64
00:04:27.551 --> 00:04:32.313
But as long as the air isn't too humid, we can make snow even when it's too warm

65
00:04:32.353 --> 00:04:33.673
for snow to fall naturally.

66
00:04:34.934 --> 00:04:37.275
If it's too humid, the water won't freeze.

67
00:04:38.655 --> 00:04:42.777
Artificial snow has even been used in the Winter Olympics since 1980.

68
00:04:44.298 --> 00:04:49.440
The majority of the snow used in the Winter Olympics in Sochi and Pyeongchang was man-made."

69
00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:55.503
"Artificial snow makes a better surface for winter sports," Zinnia said.

70
00:04:55.523 --> 00:05:01.386
"It's more durable and consistent than real snow—it doesn't clump together.

71
00:05:02.707 --> 00:05:07.310
Plus, without artificial snow, we wouldn't be able to go skiing all winter long."

72
00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:14.354
"Not only that," Aunt Kate added, "we wouldn't be able to pay our employees' wages.

73
00:05:15.515 --> 00:05:17.456
The town's economy depends on us.

74
00:05:18.904 --> 00:05:22.327
We don't just have people grooming the slopes and operating the snowmakers.

75
00:05:23.768 --> 00:05:30.634
We also hire people to operate chairlifts, monitor the slopes, and provide skiing and snowboarding lessons."

76
00:05:30.654 --> 00:05:34.357
"Hey!" Zinnia said.

77
00:05:34.377 --> 00:05:37.380
"Where are the snowcats going?"

78
00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:41.743
Kai looked down and saw the vehicles driving farther down the mountain.

79
00:05:41.763 --> 00:05:47.148
"They're finished grooming Cliffhanger," Aunt Kate said.

80
00:05:48.256 --> 00:05:49.176
"You two have fun.

81
00:05:49.556 --> 00:05:50.237
I'll see you later!"

82
00:05:53.317 --> 00:05:55.098
Zinnia looked at Kai and grinned.

83
00:05:55.998 --> 00:05:59.359
"Ready for your first really big thrill here in Colorado?"

84
00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:02.300
Kai looked down the slope again.

85
00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:08.502
Part of him wished Cliffhanger was still closed—then he wouldn't have to ski down it.

86
00:06:10.122 --> 00:06:11.823
But another part of him was excited.

87
00:06:12.723 --> 00:06:14.363
Kai adjusted his ski goggles.

88
00:06:14.383 --> 00:06:16.024
"Let's do it!"
